{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SbwnptsJDARB4RwzVmukUW","type":"tool_result","content":"total 104\ndrwxrwxr-x   2 al   al     160 Sep 26 17:04 .\ndrwxrwxrwt 109 root root  7600 Sep 26 18:01 ..\n-rw-rw-r--   1 al   al   12779 Sep 26 17:04 body.md\n-rw-rw-r--   1 al   al    2059 Sep 26 16:34 head.md\n-rw-rw-r--   1 al   al    2813 Sep 26 17:04 progress_body.md\n-rw-rw-r--   1 al   al     889 Sep 26 16:44 progress_head.md\n-rw-rw-r--   1 al   al   70292 Sep 26 17:02 tables_partial.md\n-rw-rw-r--   1 al   al     847 Sep 26 17:01 tree_partial.txt\n--- body\n\n**Method.** For every certified campaign instance at 10–75 customers\n(44,547: `results.csv` at 10–40, `results_upward.csv` at 50–75), every\ncertified corpus instance at 9–100 (6,349) and the fifteen 125 × 125 corpus\ninstances whose `default` refutation is on record as settled\n(`Random-125-125-6/8/10`; the `-2` and `-4` classes need a day each and are\nout of scope): `decide(optimum − 1)` (the refutation) and `decide(optimum)`\n(the witness search) under two arms, back to back in one process — **off**\n(`decide`'s defaults, the `default` configuration) and **on**\n(`better_move=True, better_move_dominators=0`, which is the `csearch`\nconfiguration with its switch forced) — the `sat` side's closing order\nsimulated, deadlines 60 s per call on the campaign and the corpus to 40, 120 s\nat 41–100, 2,400 s at 125. Every count is a **post-fix** count (§18 (e)).\nThe paired statistic is `(nodes_on + 1) / (nodes_off + 1)`; a censored call is\na lower bound and its pair is counted but not pooled. Five rules are scored by\nthe nodes they would have spent — *always off*, *always on*, the *hand\nthreshold* (`sparse_enough_for_better_move`, on iff mean products per\ncustomer ≤ 5), a *learned boundary* (a depth-3 decision tree on seventeen\nsize-free features — products per customer, customers per product, matrix\ndensity, `log(m/n)`, graph density, degree mean over `n`, degree dispersion,\ndegeneracy over `n`, min-fill treewidth over `n`, `optimum / n`, largest\ncomponent fraction, log components, clustering, dominated- and\ndistinct-column fractions, row and column dispersion — fitted to the *sign*\nof the paired difference with `|log10 ratio|` as the sample weight, predicted\nout of fold with folds grouped by file ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class), and\nthe *oracle* (the cheaper arm, the floor). The one-statistic threshold is also\nswept from 0 to 20. The clock is measured on the same pairs where it can be\n(refutations with ≥ 10⁵ off-nodes): per-node overhead of the on arm, seconds\nratio, and the same rules scored in seconds; a second tree is fitted to the\nsign of the seconds difference on those instances. **Baseline**: the hand\nthreshold, which is what `csearch` and `recertify` run today, and `default`.\n\n### The run\n\n50,911 instances, 203,644 decision calls, **4.4 core-hours**, 62 min on 16\nworkers (the last 30 of which were one instance, `Random-125-125-6-5_0`, whose\ntwo refutations take 1,200–1,700 s each). **Audit, all clean**: the two arms\nagree on the status of every one of the 50,4xx refutation pairs and 50,4xx\nwitness pairs where both settled (0 disagreements), no contradiction between\nthe sides, every `sat` witness under either arm simulates to at most the\noptimum (0 bad of 101,8xx), 44 refutations censored under off and 39 under on\n(all at n ≥ 75, the same instances item 06 censored). The **off arm equals\nthe recorded `default` count on 50,363 of 50,363** instances that have one\n(`results.csv`, `results_upward.csv`, `node_counts.csv`, `scale_nodes.csv`)\nand item 06's `nodes_lo_default_index` on 50,433 of 50,433; the **on arm\nequals item 06's post-fix `csearch` count on 20,932 of 20,932** instances\nwhere the hand rule was on, and its `hi` counts likewise. So the two arms are\nexactly the two configurations on record, and the new information in this\nstudy is the on arm on the **29,515 instances the hand threshold leaves off**,\nwhere Theorem 2 had never been run.\n\n*(A correction to the record: this run is the first to measure the fixed\n`better_move` on dense instances, so every statement below about\n`hand_on = False` is new; the `hand_on = True` rows reproduce §20's\n`csearch` arm and §18 (e)'s post-fix identities exactly.)*\n\n### (a) In nodes, Theorem 2 never costs where the hand rule turns it off, and rarely where it turns it on\n\nRefutation, decided pairs, `(nodes_on + 1) / (nodes_off + 1)`; the full table\nby size band and switch is in `reports/theorem2_tables.md`:\n\n| | instances | saves | ties | costs | median | p10 | p90 | max | total on/off |\n|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|\n| hand on (ppc ≤ 5) | 20,941 | 14,7xx | 6,0xx | **174** | 0.92 | 0.78 | 1.00 | 1.27 | **0.722** |\n| hand off (ppc > 5) | 29,515 | 8,8xx | 20,6xx | **1** | 1.00 | 0.93 | 1.00 | 1.012 | **0.935** |\n| all | 50,456 | 23,552 | 26,729 | 175 | 1.00 | 0.83 | 1.00 | 1.27 | 0.807 |\n\nWhere the hand rule already turns Theorem 2 on, the fixed rule saves 28% of\nthe nodes in total, 8% at the median, and **costs on 174 instances (0.8%)**\n— none of them heavy (median 247 off-nodes, 23 of the 2,614 pairs with ≥ 10⁴\noff-nodes, worst 1.27×, `ens_b_n50_m100_p0.025_i033` at 10,500 → 13,300).\n§13's \"never costs a node\" (0 of 622 pre-fix) is no longer true after the\nfix, and the p90 of 1.00 is where the change shows. Where the hand rule turns\nit **off**, forced on **saves on 8,8xx instances, ties on 20,6xx and costs on\none** (405 → 410 nodes, `ratio` 1.012), for 6.5% of the nodes in total; the\nsaving is 2–8% at the median in every band from 50 customers up (median\nratio 0.975 / 0.980 / 0.986 at 50 / 60 / 75; 0.98–0.99 in the corpus at\n41–100; 0.945 on `Random-125-125-6`, 0.991 on `-8`, 0.994 on `-10`) and\ngrows with the instance's size at fixed density — the fraction of hand-off\nrefutations whose count changes at all rises from 0.6% at n = 10 to 77% at\n50–75 and 100% above 60. By products per customer (n ≥ 30) the median ratio\nclimbs smoothly from 0.90 at ≤ 3 to 0.92 at 4–5, 0.945 at 5–6, 0.967 at 6–8,\n0.99 at 8–10 and 1.00 above 10, **never crossing 1** — there is no density at\nwhich the rule prunes fewer nodes. The realised-density table says the same\nin `col_mean`: at every size the `≥ 10` band is all ties and every other band\nis saves or ties.\n\n**The rules in nodes.** Decided pairs, totals over the whole study\n(5.4 × 10⁹ off-nodes):\n\n| rule | nodes / oracle | agreement with oracle |\n|:--|--:|--:|\n| always off (`default`) | 1.240 | 53% |\n| hand threshold (`csearch`) | 1.032 | 82% |\n| learned boundary (out of fold) | 1.000 | 99.6% |\n| **always on** | **1.0000** | 99.6% |\n| oracle | 1 | 100% |\n\nAlways on is the oracle to four decimals in every size band and both\ncollections — the 175 instances it loses on cost 0.02% of the study's nodes\n— and the hand threshold leaves 3.2% on the table (1.0–1.06 by band; 1.07 on\nthe hand-off half, 1.00 on the hand-on half). The one-statistic sweep says\nwhere: raising the threshold from 5 to 7 takes the regret from 1.03 to 1.00,\nand every threshold from 7 up is indistinguishable from always on because\nevery instance above 7 products per customer is a tie or a saving.\n\n### (b) The learned boundary is \"always on\", and the kill is not met\n\nThe depth-3 tree, fitted out of fold on the sign of the difference with the\nmagnitude as weight, predicts **on for every one of the 50,500 instances**:\nits eight leaves are all class 1 with weight ratios of 30:1 to 6,000:1 (the\ntree is printed in `theorem2_tables.md`; the splits it chose — `opt_frac`,\ncustomers per product, column dispersion — separate degrees of saving, not\nsign). Agreement with the hand threshold is therefore the hand rule's own on\nfraction, **41.5%** (0 instances hand-on/learned-off, 29,515\nhand-off/learned-on), the same with `n` added as a feature and the same on\ndecided pairs only. **Kill (plan 2 §2.5b, \"the learned boundary agrees with\nthe hand threshold on more than 95% of instances\"): not met.** The\nhypothesis of plan 2 finding 8 — that Theorem 2 should simply always be on —\nis what the nodes say, on 50,456 decided pairs at 9–125 customers.\n\n### (c) On the clock, the hand threshold is right\n\nTheorem 2 costs per node. On the 1,143 refutations with ≥ 10⁵ off-nodes, the\non arm's seconds per node are **1.17× the off arm's at the median** (p10\n1.10, p90 1.23; 1.09–1.19 by size band, 1.12–1.21 by density, flat in `n`),\nso the break-even node ratio is **0.86**: Theorem 2 pays on the clock only\nwhere it prunes more than 14% of the nodes. Where the hand rule is on, it\ndoes so in total (seconds on/off **0.91**, the big savers dominate) but not\nat the median instance (seconds ratio 1.055; on is faster on 141 of 794).\nWhere the hand rule is off, it prunes 3–7% and costs 17% per node: **on is\nslower on 347 of 349 heavy instances**, 1.13× at the median and in total.\nScoring the rules in seconds on these 1,143 pairs:\n\n| rule | seconds | / seconds floor |\n|:--|--:|--:|\n| always off | 3,844 | 1.061 |\n| **hand threshold (≤ 5)** | **3,666** | **1.012** |\n| threshold ≤ 4 | 3,743 | 1.033 |\n| threshold ≤ 6 | 3,743 | 1.033 |\n| always on | 3,914 | 1.081 |\n| floor (cheaper arm) | 3,622 | 1 |\n\n**Five is the best one-statistic threshold on the clock**, better than 4 or\n6, and always on is *worse than always off* in seconds (1.08 against 1.06),\nwhich is the opposite of what the nodes say. A tree fitted to the sign of the\n*seconds* difference on the heavy pairs agrees with the hand rule on 52% of\nthem and disagrees only in the other direction (551 hand-on / learned-off, 0\nhand-off / learned-on): it turns Theorem 2 on for matrix density ≤ 0.03 and\notherwise only where the mean degree is small and `optimum / n` large. It is","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55422e9b-f395-41d4-8bf8-6906cddbd797","uuid":"db753540-81e3-4c30-a866-1d0ce142441a","timestam