{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M6UNTriRBmkVQWrAu4roXw","type":"tool_result","content":"otherwise only where the mean degree is small and `optimum / n` large. It is\nnot offered as a rule; the seconds it was fitted on were taken on a machine\nrunning twenty processes, its 52% is not a measurement of the hand rule's\nerror, and the hand threshold beats it on the one thing that matters, total\nseconds on the heavy instances.\n\n### (d) The witness search\n\n`decide(optimum)`, decided pairs: on saves on 8,290, ties on 40,955, costs on\n1,253, total on/off 0.75 (0.74 hand-on, 0.96 hand-off). The costs are larger\nand more frequent than on the refutation — max 184× (`hand_on`, n = 75) and\n1.5× (`hand_off`) — because a dominance rule changes *which* branch is tried\nfirst as well as how many are cut, and on the satisfiable side the first\nbranch is what matters (§20 (b) found the same for the fan order). In\nseconds the on arm is 1.10–1.19× per node here too. Always on is again the\nnode oracle to 1.00–1.01 by band; the hand rule 1.00–1.06.\n\n### Proposed rule — stated, not applied\n\n- **For `decide` refutations scored in nodes: always on** (0 of 29,515\n  hand-off instances lose more than 1.2%, and a 6.5% total saving on them).\n  **For refutations scored in seconds, which is what a five-day\n  `recertify` budget is: keep the hand threshold at 5.** It is the best\n  single threshold measured on the clock, and always on would cost 8% of the\n  wall clock on the dense half. The two answers differ because Theorem 2's\n  per-node overhead (1.17×) exceeds its pruning (3–7%) exactly where the hand\n  rule turns it off. **Recommendation: no change to `sparse_enough_for_better_move`.**\n- If the C's `better_move` were made cheaper — it is one more O(R) pass over\n  data the node already holds, and `reports/inner_loop.md` shows the rules\n  *are* the inner loop — the node result says the threshold should then\n  rise; at overhead ≤ 1.05 every band from 50 customers up is on the clock's\n  right side. That is a measurement for whoever touches the C, not a rule.\n- The witness side is not where the switch matters: 1.5% of a descent's\n  nodes at 75 (§18 (b)), with a heavier tail on than off.\n\n**Size range.** Campaign 10–75 customers (44,547 instances, both generators,\n`m ∈ {n/2, n, 2n}`), corpus 9–100 (6,349) and the three dense 125 × 125\nclasses (15). The 125 × 125 `-2` and `-4` classes are not covered: their\n`default` refutations censor at 1,500 s and their `csearch` counts on record\nare pre-fix (§18 (e)); on them the hand rule is on, and the on-arm saving at\n100 on the same ridge (0.59–0.70 on `Random-100-50-4`, 0.79–0.88 at the\ndeadline on the censored `Random-100-100-2/4` pairs, where the ratio of\ncounts at equal wall clock is the per-node rate ratio, not the saving) is the\nnearest evidence. The clock statements rest on 1,143 pairs at 35–125 customers\ntimed on a loaded machine; the two arms of a pair ran back to back in one\nprocess, so the load is shared within a pair but not across pairs.\n\n**Method notes.** The `csearch` configuration *is* `default` on every\ninstance the hand rule leaves off, so the 29,515 `nodes_lo_csearch_index`\ncounts item 06 recorded there carry no information about Theorem 2 — the on\narm had to be run. A censored pair's node ratio at equal wall clock is the\ninverse per-node rate ratio (0.78–0.88 on the eleven censored 100-customer\npairs, the same 1.15–1.25× overhead the settled heavy pairs show) and must not\nbe read as a saving. Heaviest-first ordering left one worker running a\n125 × 125 instance alone for 30 of the run's 62 minutes; two workers per\n125 × 125 instance, or the large instances in their own pool, would have\nfinished in 35.\n=== head.md\n\n## 21. Theorem 2's switch: where should `better_move` be on? (plan 2 §2.5(b), loop0003 item 07)\n\n*2026-09-26. Code: `learning/theorem2.py`. No flag was added and no default\nchanged: the two arms are `decide`'s defaults and\n`decide(better_move=True, better_move_dominators=0)`, both of which already\nexist. Regenerate:*\n\n```bash\npython -m learning.theorem2 --workers 16          # the study: 50,911 instances, ~RUNMIN min on 16 cores\npython -m learning.theorem2 --stage tables        # reports/theorem2_tables.md from the CSV\npython -m pytest tests/test_theorem2.py -q\n```\n\n*Writes `learning/data/ensemble/theorem2.csv.gz` (one wide row per instance:\nstatus, nodes and seconds of the four decision calls, both witness values,\nthe hand statistic; committed), `learning/data/ensemble/theorem2_tree.txt`\n(the fitted boundary) and `reports/theorem2_tables.md` (every table below in\nfull). Nothing is written to `solutions/`.*\n\n**Question.** The `csearch` configuration turns Chu & Stuckey's Theorem 2\n(`better_move`, every candidate a dominator) on when the mean number of\nproducts per customer is at most 5 — `sparse_enough_for_better_move`, a hand\nthreshold chosen to reproduce the Chu & Stuckey density parameter from the\nmatrix — and `default` never turns it on. §13 measured the rule saving 10–13%\nof a refutation's nodes at n ≤ 40 and §14 up to 83% at n = 100, never costing\na node; §16 (b) found the switch on for nearly every `m = n/2` instance and\nsaving the same 15% wherever it is on. Plan 2 finding 8 turned that into a\nhypothesis: *it should simply always be on*. Three things were never\nmeasured. Every one of those counts was made before the 2026-09-26 fix of the\nC `better_move` (§18 (e)), which changed the rule's pruning; the rule was only\never run *where the hand threshold turns it on*, so nothing is known about\nthe instances it leaves off; and the threshold itself was never fitted. This\nitem measures Theorem 2 on against off on every instance, paired, under the\nfixed C, fits the boundary where on wins, and scores the rules.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55422e9b-f395-41d4-8bf8-6906cddbd797","uuid":"d9db9102-2be3